  "9b26743e-ea92-4d88-b92d-4446ba5b8a98": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p3-theory",
     "replacement": "An **exponential relationship** $y=a^x$, where $a>0,\\ a\\ne1$, has an exponential curve as its graph.\n\nExponential curves have these features:\n\n- **An asymptote**: a line the curve approaches. An exponential curve never reaches its horizontal asymptote.\n- The nature of the graph (**growth** or **decay**) depends on the base and the sign of the exponent, as shown below.\n- For $y=a^x$, each increase of 1 in $x$ multiplies $y$ by the **common ratio**, which is the base number $a$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p3-theory",
     "targetFingerprint": "2764141e5dde03ea9225787f79fbe5e08fbae432f9e75e9dcc22602e1c9eb84d",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-exponential-and-logarithmic-functions-1-exponential-functions-v1/20261111-035141",
   "revision": 12
  },
  "ae3b7340-3590-409f-a519-124118712113": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p7-intro-block",
     "replacement": "**Consider the equation of an exponential curve** $y=k(a)^x+c$, where $a>0,\\ a\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$.\n\nThe **parameters** $a$, $c$ and $k$ are numbers that change the graph in predictable ways.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p7-intro-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "7d4f3ba4372814cbd7a2b3755836c81f723ada44cec33f976999e21fd0b2cb95",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-exponential-and-logarithmic-functions-1-exponential-functions-v1/20261111-035141",
   "revision": 12
  },
  "031b31b5-f171-49a2-9f2e-3021146f3f6c": {
